I had a mini-stroke like thing when I was like 14. Apparently the muscles in my back were constricting the blood flow to one side of my brain. I was doing a life guard exam and was in the water at the time it started. First half of my body got major pins and needles, including my mouth and tongue, so speech was greatly impaired. And that's if you could even think of the words to say. I remember trying to phone my house phone but couldn't even remember how many digits where in a phone number. Started walking to the hospital. On the way, I remember that I could call my mum's mobile and went through my contacts to call her. Said I was going to the place that begins with "H" and she found me curled up outside the front of the hospital by the road. Next 4 hours were spent with what I imagine cluster headaches are and dry-heaving and spitting out stomach lining.

&amp;#x200B;

There's a really good TED talk on what its like to have a stroke by a woman who is a brain scientist and has had a stroke, I'm afraid I can't remember her name. Some of the experiences she describe sound a little bit like a mushroom trip.

She also mentions that she couldn't recognize her own hand as hers, it seemed more like it belonged to some animal. Really good anyway, check it out if you can find it.

&amp;#x200B;

EDIT: More symptoms to do with vision is like your peripheral vision just doesn't work. If you look just to the left of something, it just sort of disappears. Not like a white or black spot, just like it isn't there. I guess somehow you're brain does some re-stitching of your field of vision to make it look like it makes sense.

Stats on notebookchecker put them pretty damn similar. Some games perform better the 2070 max q, while some games get a better fps on the 2060.

&amp;#x200B;

According to PC Specialist recommendations, both the 2060 and the 2070 max are VR ready and play everything at 1440p, however, only the full fat laptop 2070 is properly capable of 4k gaming at reasonable levels.

&amp;#x200B;

I am personally going to go for the 2060, specifically in the PC Specialist RECOIL III RTX laptop.

CLEVO PB50RF / PC Specialist Vortex IX - Anyone with any experience?
Hi all,

&amp;#x200B;

I'm shopping around to get a new laptop. I bought a 4k TV ages ago (very prematurely) and would like to utilize it at last. 

This means I need, at the very least, a laptop with a RTX 2070 (non max q).

&amp;#x200B;

I've bought from PC Specialist in the past and been very happy with them, so will probably buy from them again.

They recently brought out a new laptop line up which includes the Vortex IX, which can be configured to have a 2070 in.

&amp;#x200B;

I was looking at the Recoil III with the RTX 2060 or RTX 2070 Max Q, which is about £300 cheaper, but after seeing the keyboard in a review, its a no go just based on that, leaving me with the Vortex.

&amp;#x200B;

My problem is this:

&amp;#x200B;

No one has one.

&amp;#x200B;

There are no reviews for this chassis. From the pictures, the keyboard looks very decent, but I would like to know about noise and temps. As well as get some bench marks. 

&amp;#x200B;

There is nothing out there.

&amp;#x200B;

If anyone has any experience, or just knows where I can find some hands on reviews, or some other alternatives, I would be incredibly grateful.

This is the only other place I've found talking about the Clevo machine:

